/** @page natsarchguide Architecture

    @tableofcontents

    The module has three layers:

    - <b>Binary module (\c nats.so)</b>: C++ QPP wrapping \c libnats for connection
      management, publish/subscribe, request/reply, JetStream, and Key-Value store.
      Provides @ref Qore::Nats::NatsConnection, @ref Qore::Nats::NatsSubscription,
      @ref Qore::Nats::JetStreamContext, and @ref Qore::Nats::NatsKeyValueStore.
    - <b>Qore module (\c NatsUtil)</b>: Pure %Qore providing high-level convenience
      classes. Provides @ref Nats::NatsClient, @ref Nats::NatsChannelSubscription,
      and @ref Nats::NatsHelper.
    - <b>Data provider modules</b>: @ref natsdataproviderintro "NatsDataProvider" and
      @ref natsjetstreamdataproviderintro "NatsJetStreamDataProvider" for integration with Qore's data
      provider framework.

    @section natscallbacksandbox Callbacks and Sandboxing

    The NATS C client calls the connection callbacks (\c on_disconnect, \c on_reconnect, \c on_closed,
    \c on_error, \c on_lame_duck and \c on_discovered_servers in @ref Qore::Nats::NatsConnectionOptions) and the
    request handlers of microservice endpoints on its own threads.  Each callback runs under the sandbox
    (\c Qore::SandboxManager) of the code that registered it: the code that created the connection for the
    connection callbacks, and the code that added the endpoint for a request handler.  A callback can therefore never
    do more than the code that registered it, although it runs on a thread that %Qore did not start.

    The NATS C client also reconnects on its own thread, to the configured servers and to the servers that the
    cluster reports (discovered servers).  When a sandbox governs the code that creates a connection, every
    connection that the client makes for it, the first one and each reconnection, is made by the module under that
    sandbox: the host name is resolved and each address is checked against the network policy before it is connected
    to.  A reconnection to a server that the policy denies, for example a discovered server on another network, fails
    like a reconnection to a server that is down.

    @section natsfork Child Processes Created with fork()

    A child process created with \c fork() runs only the thread that called \c fork(): the threads of the NATS C
    client (its library threads, which run timers, callbacks and garbage collection, and the read loop, flusher and
    reconnect threads of each connection) remain in the parent, and the child shares the sockets of the parent's
    connections.  The NATS objects of the parent therefore cannot be used in the child, and the C client cannot be
    used there either; a child process that needs NATS must start a new process (ex: by calling \c exec() in the
    child).

    When the child destroys a NATS object of its parent, explicitly or when it ends its script, or closes a
    connection of its parent, the object is abandoned without calling the C client, which would wait forever for
    the parent's threads or write to the parent's connection; the module's cleanup in the child likewise leaves the
    C client library as it is.  The parent's connections, subscriptions and microservices are not affected.

    A child process created after the C client was used ends without running the exit-time handlers of the libraries
    in the process (with %Qore 3.0 or later, see \c qore_fork_child_exit_quickly()): the C client's own exit handler
    takes a lock that a thread of the client in the parent can have held when the process was created.

    @section natscancellation Thread Cancellation

    A blocking call ends with \c THREAD-CANCELLED when its thread is cancelled, or with \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED
    when its \c Program is interrupted:
    - @ref Qore::Nats::NatsConnection::request() "NatsConnection::request()" ends at once
    - @ref Qore::Nats::NatsSubscription::nextMsg() "NatsSubscription::nextMsg()",
      @ref Qore::Nats::NatsSubscription::fetch() "NatsSubscription::fetch()",
      @ref Qore::Nats::NatsSubscription::waitForDrainCompletion() "NatsSubscription::waitForDrainCompletion()",
      @ref Qore::Nats::NatsConnection::flush() "NatsConnection::flush()" and
      @ref Qore::Nats::NatsKeyValueWatcher::next() "NatsKeyValueWatcher::next()" end within about 500 ms: the NATS
      C client offers no way to wake these waits, so they wait in slices of at most 500 ms
*/
